finding":"The plan's core causal ordering — 'gerrit_healthy (later still)' after mem_used_percent — is contradicted by the actual script: gerrit_healthy is published near the TOP (line 97, §1 Health probes) while mem_used_percent is published near the END (line 1020, §2d). Under the plan's own 'position decides survival' mechanism, an early metric like gerrit_healthy should SURVIVE a mid-run kill, so 'no datapoints at all' cannot be explained by lateness.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f907a0bf44b00713a","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Section 'Why this is the root cause of several open tickets, not a new symptom' — the ordered bullet list (root_disk early / mem later / gerrit later still)","norm_id":"nbf585ae591c76399","priority":0.9,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Diagnosis is acted on and the fix reorders/decouples sections, but gerrit_healthy's true absence has a different root cause (e.g. a curl --max-time 10 stall at line 89 or an early exit) that remains unfixed"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Re-derive each cited metric's actual line/section position from observability.sh and either correct the ordering narrative or identify the separate mechanism (e.g. the early curl health-probe at line 89 blocking, or a distinct failure) responsible for gerrit_healthy having zero datapoints.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.9,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"high","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Reconcile the publication-ordering claim with observability.sh: gerrit_healthy publishes at line ~97 (before mem_used_percent at ~1020), so either correct the ordering evidence or explain gerrit_healthy's total absence by a mechanism other than late-section truncation.","cohort":["G1G2"],"criteria":["G1G2"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.1,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: `gerrit_healthy` (later still) — no datapoints at all","observability.sh:97 `--metric-name gerrit_healthy` is the earliest of the three cited metrics","observability.sh:1020 `--metric-name mem_used_percent` is near the end","observability.sh has only one gerrit_healthy publish site (line 97); no later re-publish"],"finding":"The plan asserts publication ordering `root_disk_used_percent (early)` -> `mem_used_percent (later)` -> `gerrit_healthy (later still)`, but in the actual edit target infra/scripts/observability.sh gerrit_healthy is published FIRST (line 97), well before root_disk_used_percent (line 192) and mem_used_percent (line 1020). Under the plan's own sequential-truncation mechanism gerrit_healthy would be the MOST likely metric to survive a mid-run SIGTERM, yet the plan cites it as having 'no datapoints at all' — the strongest evidence of late-section starvation. The named symbol's position in the target file contradicts the plan's causal model.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc4e5411da21a20e3","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"\"Why this is the root cause\" section, bullets on root_disk/mem/gerrit ordering","norm_id":"n3972876e8ce73f56","priority":0.9,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Reviewer/implementer follows the plan's ordering map to predict which metrics fall after the kill point and finds gerrit_healthy is early, breaking the diagnosis","AC 'Confirm which metrics fall after the kill point and that they match the observed absences' fails because gerrit_healthy's absence is not explained by position"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Re-derive the per-section ordering from observability.sh line numbers and either pick genuinely late metrics as the truncation examples or add the separate mechanism (e.g. gerrit health-check curl hanging/failing) that explains gerrit_healthy having no datapoints despite its early position.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.9,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"high","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.8,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Reconcile the LEAD with the code: every du/df in observability.sh is already `timeout`-wrapped, so the overrun is the SUM of bounded calls (two 120s docker du calls alone reach the 240s TimeoutStartSec) — reframe AC1 to measure cumulative section time against a global budget rather than to find a single blocking call.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.1,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["observability.sh:515 `raw=\"$(timeout \"$DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT\" du -sx --block-size=1 \"$1\" ...`","observability.sh:323 `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT=\"${DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT:-120}\"` — and it is called TWICE (lines 592-593: DOCKER_ROOT and DOCKER_ROOT/overlay2)","observability.sh:540,703,788 — df, journal, var_tmp du are ALSO timeout-wrapped (20/60/60s)","install-observability.sh:41 `TimeoutStartSec=240`","Plan LEAD: 'docker_du_bytes runs `du -sx` ... is exactly a multi-minute blocking call ... name the blocking call'"],"finding":"The plan's stated prime-suspect mechanism — that docker_du_bytes's `du -sx` is a singular multi-minute blocking call to be 'named' — is contradicted by the code, which already bounds every du/df with `timeout`. The true overrun mechanism is cumulative: individually-bounded calls with no aggregate wall-clock budget, which no AC addresses.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fa08b630f8501edab","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Plan sections 'The decisive measurement' / 'Prime suspect, stated as a LEAD' and AC1","norm_id":"nb0bfc5fc912b7ed9","priority":0.9,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Implementer chases a single unbounded du that does not exist because all du calls are already timeout-bounded","Two docker du calls at 120s each already sum to the entire 240s budget before journal/var_tmp sections even run, so per-section timing 'names' several bounded calls, not one"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"State that the diagnosis must account for existing per-call `timeout` bounds and identify whether total bounded time can exceed 240s, not just which single call is slowest.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.9,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"